description:
Verify the caller's Firebase ID token with jose instead of the Admin SDK. Same tokens, same Google signing keys, ZERO client changes.

  const JWKS = createRemoteJWKSet(new URL('https://www.googleapis.com/service_accounts/v1/jwk/securetoken@system.gserviceaccount.com'));
  const { payload } = await jwtVerify(token, JWKS, {
    issuer: `https://securetoken.google.com/${PROJECT_ID}`,
    audience: PROJECT_ID,
    algorithms: ['RS256'],
  });
  return payload.sub;

SECURITY: algorithms: ['RS256'] must be pinned. Without it a JWT library can be tricked into accepting alg: none or an HMAC forged with the public key. Also reject an empty/missing sub.

jwtVerify checks signature, exp, nbf, iss, aud, and alg - exactly what verifyIdToken(token, false) checks. createRemoteJWKSet fetches once per instance, caches, and handles Google's key rotation. Keep the existing bearer-parsing and 401 error shape from api/_lib/firebase-admin.ts:41-51 (Object.assign(new Error(...), { status: 401 })).

ACCEPTED DOWNGRADE: no revocation check. Today's verifyIdToken(token, true) asks Firebase whether the session was revoked; jose cannot. A signed-out or disabled account keeps working until its current token expires, <=1h. Leave a ponytail: comment naming the ceiling and the upgrade path (one Auth REST lookup per request).

muscle-analysis is the only op still importing app code: api/_lib/ops.ts:53-55 builds a region list from MUSCLE_REGIONS/muscleLabel (constants/muscles.ts). That file has 15+ app importers so it cannot move to shared/.

Move those three lines verbatim into utils/muscle-analysis.ts, which already imports constants/muscles and already computes and sends volumeTable from the same source. Send it as a second input field. The server keeps owning the prompt template; regionList is data, exactly like volumeTable.

  const regionList = Object.entries(MUSCLE_REGIONS)
    .map(([region, muscles]) => `${region}: ${muscles.map(muscleLabel).join(', ')}`)
    .join('\\n');

Server side: the prompt in ai/prompts.ts interpolates input.regionList where it used to interpolate the locally-built string. Prompt text itself is unchanged.

apps/api/src/worker.ts:124-134. The console.warn that records why App Check failed sits BELOW the enforce throw, so it is dead code in enforce mode - the one mode where you need to know why a 401 happened is the one mode that prints nothing.

1. Move the warn above the throw:
   const appCheck = await verifyAppCheckToken(context.req.header('X-Firebase-AppCheck'), context.env);
   // Logged before the throw: under enforce this is the only signal that says
   // *why* a 401 happened. Still never logs the token, the body, or the uid.
   if (!appCheck.verified) console.warn('[worker] app-check-unverified', { reason: appCheck.reason, route: context.req.path });
   if (context.env.APP_CHECK_MODE === 'enforce' && !appCheck.verified) {
     throw new ApiError(401, 'Invalid or missing App Check token', 'app_check_failed');
   }
   Keep the 401 body generic. Do NOT put reason in the response - the five reasons (missing, misconfigured, wrong_type, wrong_app, invalid) tell a caller how close a forged token got. The wrangler dev terminal is the right place for them.

2. verifyAppCheckToken is a hard module import at worker.ts:6, so no worker test can drive the enforce SUCCESS path - the existing enforce test (worker.test.ts:74-77) only covers 'missing'. Add a second parameter mirroring the existing verifyUid one:
   export function createWorkerApp(verifyUid: VerifyUid = requireUid, verifyAppCheck = verifyAppCheckToken)

3. Using that seam, add worker.test.ts cases: a VERIFIED App Check token under APP_CHECK_MODE 'enforce' reaches the route normally (not 401); an unverified one still 401s with code app_check_failed. Keep the base env on monitor so the tombstone assertions at worker.test.ts:86-90 are unaffected. Bare bun + node:assert/strict, matching the file style.

The enforce-success path is the entire risk of this change - that enforce rejects LEGITIMATE traffic - and it is currently untested.

